        },
        "asb_slowerFraction": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "avgspeedbin_rows",
            "runspecsourcetype_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The weight the slower of the two bracketing schedules contributes, drivecycle.rs:127-142. The faster one contributes 1 minus this in every branch, which is why lib/drive_cycle.esm carries one template and not two."
        },
        "asb_scheduleFraction": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "avgspeedbin_rows",
            "drive_schedule_assoc_rows",
            "runspecsourcetype_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The bracketing weight attached to the schedule that carries it. The two bracketing schedules are identified by SPEED rather than by an argmax over ids, so a bin whose bracket speed were shared by two selected schedules would get both -- which is exactly what asb_scheduleFractionTotal catches."
        },
        "asb_scheduleFractionTotal": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "avgspeedbin_rows",
            "runspecsourcetype_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Must be 1 for every bin. This is the window check finding F17's `tech_fractionTotal` is: a duplicated bracket speed or a bin with no bracket shows up as a number other than 1 rather than as a missing row."
        },
        "asb_modeFraction": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "avgspeedbin_rows",
            "operating_mode_rows",
            "physicsrow_rows"
          ],
          "description": "drivecycle.rs:388-401: the schedule-fraction-weighted combination, i.e. the operating-mode distribution of one average-speed bin FOR ONE VEHICLE. The two factors sit on different axes -- the bracketing weight is per (source type, road type) because it is a property of the schedules, the mode distribution is per vehicle because it is a property of the physics -- and `phy_sourceTypeOrdinal` is the join that puts them on one row. This is the VSP/braking family; `asb_tireModeFraction` is the other, and it is per speed bin alone."
        },
        "asb_tireModeFraction": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "avgspeedbin_rows",
            "operating_mode_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The tire-wear operating-mode distribution of one average-speed bin: 1 at the mode `opModeIDTirewear` names and 0 everywhere else. avg_speed_op_mode_distribution.rs:49-53 -- 'tire-wear operating-mode distributions are degenerate: every emitted row carries opModeFraction = 1'. It is a `join.on` between `asb_opModeIDTirewear` and `om_opModeID` rather than an `==` in a filter, so the 1 is the additive identity of a matched row and the 0 is the additive identity of an unmatched one (docs/esm-conventions.md 3, 20.4)."
        },
        "day_ordinal": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "runspecday_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The day relation's own row ordinal, so derived relations join to it by position."
        },
        "month_ordinal": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "runspecmonth_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The month relation's own row ordinal, so derived relations join to it by position."
        },
        "rsc_ordinal": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "runspeccounty_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The county relation's own row ordinal, so the output relation joins to it by POSITION -- the same construction `day_ordinal` is, one axis up."
        },
        "day_hourDayID": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "runspecday_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The packed hour-day key, LOOKED UP from (dayID, hourID)."
        },
        "pp_W": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "runspecpollutantprocess_rows",
            "runspecday_rows",
            "operating_mode_rows",
            "physicsrow_rows"
          ],
          "description": "W, and IT IS KEYED BY THE POLLUTANT-PROCESS HERE -- the one structural difference between this fixture and `process-brakewear`. `BaseRateGenerator` is invoked per process and `RatesOpModeDistribution` is written by two different generators: for Running Exhaust and Brakewear it drives the drive cycles itself (mod.rs:156-161, `should_process_drive_cycles = process_id == 1 || process_id == 9`), and for Tirewear it reads what `AverageSpeedOperatingModeDistributionGenerator` wrote (avg_speed_op_mode_distribution.rs:110-116, `TIREWEAR_PROCESS = ProcessId(10)`). Both give the same shape -- Sum over the speed bins of avgSpeedFraction x opModeFraction, aggregate.rs:365-417 -- and differ only in which mode fraction they carry, so this is ONE aggregate with the mode fraction selected per pollutant-process rather than two relations and a branch downstream."
        },
        "pp_WTotal": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "runspecpollutantprocess_rows",
            "runspecday_rows",
            "physicsrow_rows"
          ],
          "description": "W summed over operating modes, per pollutant-process. It is the avgSpeedFraction column's own total on EITHER mode family -- both distributions partition the same 16 speed bins -- so it is 1 to the reference's six-significant-figure storage and not exactly, which is why its test carries an absolute tolerance rather than a relative one (docs/esm-conventions.md 20.5). That the two families give the same total is the check that the tire-wear arm is a re-binning of the same activity and not a second one."
        },
        "pp_WModeCount": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "runspecpollutantprocess_rows",
            "runspecday_rows",
            "physicsrow_rows"
          ],
          "description": "How many operating modes carry a non-zero weight, per pollutant-process. 23 on the 9101 rows -- 0, 1, 11-16, 21-25, 27-30, 33, 35 and 37-40, the drive-cycle set -- and 16 on both tire-wear rows: modes 401 to 416, one per speed bin. SIXTEEN AND NOT SEVENTEEN IS THE TIRE-WEAR ANALOGUE OF BRAKEWEAR'S 23-AND-NOT-24. Operating mode 400, `tirewear;idle`, exists in `operatingmode`, is associated with 11710 by `opmodepolprocassoc` and carries an `emissionrate` row -- but no speed bin names it, because `buildOpModeClause` assigns 400 only to a PROJECT link whose linkAvgSpeed is below 0.1 mph (avg_speed_op_mode_distribution.rs:118-125). A 17 here would mean the classification had taken the project-scale branch."
        },
        "pp_tireIdleWeight": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "runspecpollutantprocess_rows",
            "runspecday_rows",
            "physicsrow_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The weight operating mode 400 carries, stated directly rather than inferred from `pp_WModeCount`. Exactly 0, for the reason that variable's description gives. Brakewear's mode 501 needed the same statement and got it as a mode count; this fixture makes it a number as well, because tire wear's whole rate table lives in the 400 family and an idle mode inside it is easier to acquire by accident than one outside."
        },
