            "runspecmonth_rows"
          ],
          "description": "`calculate_nox_k`. Exactly one arm can be non-zero for a given fuel type, so their SUM is the selection; a fuel type with no row takes 1. Measured on `expand-criteria`: 1.0215928 for gasoline and E85, 1.0095484 for diesel."
        },
        "rt_noxTemperatureAdjust": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows",
            "runspeccounty_rows",
            "runspecmonth_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The NOx branch's temperature term. Its coefficients are 0 for every model year `expand-criteria` emits -- `temperatureadjustment` carries polProcess 301 only for model years 2027 and later -- so the term is 0 there and the whole NOx factor is the humidity correction."
        },
        "rt_standardTemperatureAdjust": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows",
            "runspeccounty_rows",
            "runspecmonth_rows"
          ],
          "description": "`general_temp_adjust`'s fall-through term, the quadratic about 75 degF that every pollutant with no branch of its own takes. It is 0 for every pollutant-process any RunSpec in this tree emits, because the only rows `temperatureadjustment` carries are for pollutant-processes none of them reach -- which is exactly why adding this branch leaves all six existing runs unchanged to the digit."
        },
        "rt_temperatureFactor": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows",
            "runspeccounty_rows",
            "runspecmonth_rows"
          ],
          "description": "S15's temperature adjustment, `general_temp_adjust` (adjust.rs:95-141) in its own branch order. The EV arm was the only one this chain had, because energy and tire wear were the only pollutants it computed; the NOx arm and the fall-through arrive with the criteria pollutants. For total NOx the factor is `(1 + tempAdjust) x k` -- the humidity correction MULTIPLIES the temperature factor rather than adding to it, which is the part a reader would not guess from the table names.\n\nWHAT IS STILL MISSING, AND SAID RATHER THAN LEFT: adjust.rs's FIRST branch is PM (pollutant 112 or 118, process 1 or 2), an exponential in `72 - T` with a ceiling at 72. No RunSpec in this tree emits a PM pollutant, so it is not written here; `fixtures/process-pm-exhaust.esm` carries it and `lib/adjustments.esm` carries its templates. A PM RunSpec would take the fall-through quadratic and be wrong, which is why this is a named gap and not a silence."
        },
        "rt_batteryEfficiency": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "J33: fuelTypeID is FORCED to 9 by setup.rs:3221, so the evefficiency table has no fuel column and the gate is the age GROUP."
        },
        "rt_chargingEfficiency": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The charging half of the divisor, 0.94."
        },
        "rt_hasEvEfficiency": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Whether evefficiency carries a row for this pollutant-process at all. It does for 9101 and NOT for 11710, and the difference is load-bearing: without this test lib/onroad_activity.esm's `ev_energy_divisor` would divide the tire-wear electricity rate by its own 1e-300 floor and return an infinity."
        },
        "rt_appliesEvDivisor": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The divisor applies to an electricity cohort that HAS an evefficiency row -- the LEFT-JOIN reading of adjust.rs:611-616, and the argument `ev_energy_divisor` actually wants."
        },
        "rt_evEnergyDivisor": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "batteryEfficiency x chargingEfficiency, or 1."
        },
        "rt_emissionRateAdjustmentExact": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "J56, the lookup half. `adjust.rs:586-603` keys `EmissionRateAdjustment` by (polProcessID, sourceTypeID, regClassID, fuelTypeID, modelYearID) -- the same five as the EV efficiency divisor above it, which is why moves.rs collapses the two into one `PolProcSourceRegFuelMyKey` -- and the table states the model year as a RANGE, so four of the five are `join.on` and the fifth is lib/keys.esm's `model_year_in_range`."
        },
        "rt_hasEmissionRateAdjustment": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The existence half of J56. MOVES looks the factor up and applies it only if the key is present, so a rate row with no row is adjusted by 1 rather than by 0 -- which a sum_product lookup alone would say."
        },
        "rt_emissionRateAdjustment": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The factor `rtDay_adjustedMeanBaseRate` multiplies by: the matched row, or 1 where the table has none. THIS IS WHAT `expand-sourcetype`'S LAST THREE CELLS WERE. Source type 51 (refuse truck), fuel type 9 (electricity), model years 2017 to 2019 came out high by exactly 3.622713, which is 1 / 0.276035, the factor row 9101/51/47/9/1950-2060 holds; with it they land at 1.4e-06 of MOVESOutput. No RunSpec before that one selected a source type whose electricity is heavy-duty, so every cohort this corpus had reached missed the table and 1 was right -- which is why a table read by nothing passed four runs. docs/mixed-onroad.md §2.3(c) called the step `flag false in production` on that evidence and was wrong."
        },
        "rtDay_adjustedMeanBaseRate": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows",
            "runspecday_rows",
            "runspeccounty_rows",
            "runspecmonth_rows"
          ],
          "description": "S15 in order: the A/C increment scaled by the A/C factor, then the temperature factor, then the EV divisor. Per county: two of the three factors vary with it."
        },
        "day_noOfRealDays": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "runspecday_rows"
          ],
          "description": "dayofanyweek.noOfRealDays: 5 for the weekday type, the only one this run selects; the weekend type's 2 is not in this execution database (docs/esm-conventions.md 42). mod.rs:1584-1590 makes a missing divisor a hard error rather than a 1."
        },
        "rtDay_sho": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows",
            "runspecday_rows",
            "runspeccounty_rows",
            "runspecmonth_rows"
          ],
          "description": "J34: (hourDayID, ageID) as one clause with two key pairs. `sho` carries no dayID column, which is why the day relation derives an hourDayID. Per county, through `act_sho`."
        },
        "rtDay_activity": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows",
            "runspecday_rows",
            "runspeccounty_rows",
            "runspecmonth_rows"
          ],
          "description": "S16, aggregate.rs:223-231: universalActivity = SHO / noOfRealDays. Per county."
        },
        "rt_modelYearGroupID": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The model-year GROUP `pollutantprocessmodelyear` gives this rate row's (pollutant-process, model year). `rt_modelYearGroupIsPresent` (J18, J19) already asks whether the pair is there; this is the value, and it is a key rather than a test because `NONO2Ratio` is keyed by the group. It is 0 for every pollutant-process whose group is `Doesn't Matter`, which is most of them."
        },
        "rt_pm10ChainRatio": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "J36, PM10BrakeTireCalculator.sql: PM10PM25Ratio for the CHAINED pollutant-process, on this cohort's fuel type and source type, in the model-year range that brackets its model year (lib/keys.esm's `model_year_in_range`). Zero on the two unchained rate paths, because `pm10emissionratio` carries polProcessID 10710 and nothing else -- so the chain term vanishes there with no branch. 6.6667 on every chained row here, which is why docs/process-tirewear.md 7.2 says this fixture cannot see the fuel key or the range."
        },
        "rt_noxChainRatio": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "J79, NOCalculator and NO2Calculator: `NONO2Ratio` for the chained SPECIES -- NO, NO2 or HONO -- on this cohort's fuel type and source type, at the model-year group its own pollutant-process maps this model year to. Zero everywhere else, including on total NOx itself, which is the parent and not a species. The join has no electricity row to find, so it is also what makes a species block 104 cohorts where its parent is 124."
        },
        "rt_crankcaseChainRatio": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "J80, CrankcaseEmissionCalculator: `CrankcaseEmissionRatio` for the chained crankcase process on this cohort's fuel type, source type and regulatory class, in the model-year range that brackets its model year. Zero everywhere else."
        },
        "rt_chainRatio": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The chain ratio, whichever of the three tables holds it. The arms are DISJOINT by pollutant-process -- `PM10EmissionRatio` carries the particulate chains, `NONO2Ratio` the nitrogen species and `CrankcaseEmissionRatio` the crankcase processes, and no polProcessID is in two of them -- so the union is a sum and not a precedence."
        },
        "rt_hasPm10ChainRatio": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Does `PM10EmissionRatio` carry a row for this pollutant-process, fuel type, source type and model year? A PRESENCE test, separate from the value, because a ratio of exactly zero is a ratio."
        },
        "rt_hasNoxChainRatio": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Does `NONO2Ratio` carry a row for this species, fuel type, source type and model-year group? Zero for every electricity row, which is the whole of why a species block is 104 cohorts and its parent 124."
        },
        "rt_hasCrankcaseChainRatio": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Does `CrankcaseEmissionRatio` carry a row for this crankcase process, fuel type, source type, regulatory class and model year? It does for diesel model years 2001 and later, and the ratio there is exactly 0.0 -- a cohort that is emitted as a zero, not a cohort that is absent."
        },
        "rt_hasChainRatio": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Is this rate row chained from something, with a ratio recorded for it in any of the three tables? The union is a `max` because the arms are disjoint and a presence flag is not additive."
        },
        "rt_parentEmitsGen1": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Generation 1 of the chain's presence fixed point: does the pollutant-process this row is CHAINED FROM carry a RATE of its own on this same cohort? A self-join of the rate relation on (chained-from pollutant-process, cohort), under `max` because the cohort's source bins would otherwise be counted one per bin. THE CHAIN IS A FIXED POINT, UNROLLED THREE TIMES. A chained pollutant-process can itself be the parent of another: `chain-tog-speciation` runs THC (101) to NMHC (7901) to NMOG (8001) to TOG (8601), and `process-airtoxics` runs THC to NMHC to VOC (8701) to benzene (2001). Reading the parent's DIRECT quantity, which is what one pass does, gives a grandchild zero. Three is not a guess: it is the longest chain in the whole snapshot corpus (docs/esm-conventions.md §71), measured over every `runspecchainedto` in `../moves.rs/characterization/snapshots`, and a run deeper than three would leave its deepest species at zero rather than wrong -- `run_chainDeeperThanThree` counts exactly those rows and is asserted at 0. The three passes are spelled out rather than factored because a `faq`'s ranges and join clause are equation-level and an expression template cannot carry them; what IS shared -- the emission predicate -- is lib/emission_factors.esm's `chained_emits`, applied at all three."
        },
        "rt_emitsGen1": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "`rt_emits` as one pass of the chain would compute it: a row emits if it has a rate of its own, or if its ratio exists and its parent has a rate of its own. Correct for every chain one deep, which is every RunSpec in `runspecs/` before the hydrocarbon species."
        },
        "rt_parentEmitsGen2": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Generation 2: does the parent emit at generation 1? The same self-join, one rung further from the root."
        },
        "rt_emitsGen2": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "`rt_emits` after two passes: correct for every chain two deep."
        },
        "rt_parentEmitsHere": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Generation 3, the last: does the pollutant-process this row is CHAINED FROM emit after two passes? A species is emitted where its ratio exists AND its parent was emitted; for the particulate chains the two are the same set, for the nitrogen ones the ratio is the narrower test, and for the hydrocarbon species the parent is itself a species. THE CHAIN IS A FIXED POINT, UNROLLED THREE TIMES. A chained pollutant-process can itself be the parent of another: `chain-tog-speciation` runs THC (101) to NMHC (7901) to NMOG (8001) to TOG (8601), and `process-airtoxics` runs THC to NMHC to VOC (8701) to benzene (2001). Reading the parent's DIRECT quantity, which is what one pass does, gives a grandchild zero. Three is not a guess: it is the longest chain in the whole snapshot corpus (docs/esm-conventions.md §71), measured over every `runspecchainedto` in `../moves.rs/characterization/snapshots`, and a run deeper than three would leave its deepest species at zero rather than wrong -- `run_chainDeeperThanThree` counts exactly those rows and is asserted at 0. The three passes are spelled out rather than factored because a `faq`'s ranges and join clause are equation-level and an expression template cannot carry them; what IS shared -- the emission predicate -- is lib/emission_factors.esm's `chained_emits`, applied at all three."
        },
        "rtDay_quantDirect": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows",
            "runspecday_rows",
            "runspeccounty_rows",
            "runspecmonth_rows"
          ],
          "description": "S17 and S18: rate x activity, in the output's own units. Exactly zero on the chained pollutant-process, because `emissionrate` carries no 10710 row and the inner join of J22 leaves its rate at the additive identity. Per county."
        },
        "rtDay_chainGen1": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows",
            "runspecday_rows",
            "runspeccounty_rows",
            "runspecmonth_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The chain term computed against the parent's OWN-RATE quantity: correct for a species whose parent carries a rate, which is every chain one deep. One rung of the chain's value fixed point -- see lib/chaining.esm for why the chain is a fixed point and why three rungs is the measured maximum, and `run_chainDepthExceeded` for the per-run assertion that this run does not need a fourth."
        },
        "rtDay_quantGen1": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows",
            "runspecday_rows",
            "runspeccounty_rows",
            "runspecmonth_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The quantity after one rung: own rate plus a parent's own rate scaled. Zero on a species two rungs from a rate, which is what the next generation repairs. One rung of the chain's value fixed point -- see lib/chaining.esm for why the chain is a fixed point and why three rungs is the measured maximum, and `run_chainDepthExceeded` for the per-run assertion that this run does not need a fourth."
        },
        "rtDay_chainGen2": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows",
            "runspecday_rows",
            "runspeccounty_rows",
            "runspecmonth_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The chain term computed against the parent's quantity after one rung. One rung of the chain's value fixed point -- see lib/chaining.esm for why the chain is a fixed point and why three rungs is the measured maximum, and `run_chainDepthExceeded` for the per-run assertion that this run does not need a fourth."
        },
        "rtDay_quantGen2": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows",
            "runspecday_rows",
            "runspeccounty_rows",
            "runspecmonth_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The quantity after two rungs. One rung of the chain's value fixed point -- see lib/chaining.esm for why the chain is a fixed point and why three rungs is the measured maximum, and `run_chainDepthExceeded` for the per-run assertion that this run does not need a fourth."
        },
        "rtDay_quantChained": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows",
            "runspecday_rows",
            "runspeccounty_rows",
            "runspecmonth_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The chain term at the THIRD and last rung: the parent's quantity after two rungs, at the same source bin and day, times this row's ratio. It is a SELF-JOIN of the rate relation -- the chained row and the row it is chained from are two rows of one relation -- so the clause names its two sides with `syms` (CONFORMANCE_SPEC 5.5.8). On an unchained row the ratio is 0 and the chain key is 0, which matches no pollutant-process, so both halves say the same thing. PM10BrakeTireCalculator's whole arithmetic is the one-rung case of this. Per county. One rung of the chain's value fixed point -- see lib/chaining.esm for why the chain is a fixed point and why three rungs is the measured maximum, and `run_chainDepthExceeded` for the per-run assertion that this run does not need a fourth."
        },
        "rtDay_quant": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows",
            "runspecday_rows",
            "runspeccounty_rows",
            "runspecmonth_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The emitted quantity: its own rate path plus its chain. Every rate row is exactly one of the two, so the sum is a union and not a double count -- `run_chainedRowCount` is the test. Per county, which is the axis the output relation's second level reads it at."
        },
        "rt_parentEmitsGen4": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Generation 4, which exists only to be compared with generation 3: does the parent emit after three rungs? A presence pass carries no day, county or month, so a fourth costs one more self-join of the rate relation and nothing else."
        },
        "rt_emitsGen4": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "`rt_emits` after four rungs. Equal to `rt_emits` on every RunSpec in `runspecs/`, and that equality is the whole point: it is what turns \"three rungs is enough\" from an assumption into a measurement the run itself makes."
        },
        "run_chainDepthExceeded": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "run_rows"
          ],
          "description": "How many rate rows a FOURTH rung of the chain would newly emit: 0, asserted. lib/chaining.esm records that three is the longest chain in the whole snapshot corpus; this is the run's own check of it, and a run that broke it would leave its deepest species at zero rather than wrong -- which this counts and the shape checker would then see as missing rows."
        },
        "run_chainedRowCount": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "run_rows"
          ],
          "description": "How many emitted rate rows carry a chain ratio: 125, the one chained pollutant-process's cohorts. A rate row can never have both a ratio and a rate of its own here, which is what makes `rtDay_quant`'s sum a union."
        },
        "bin_isSelectedAt": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The bin's membership read back at the rate row, so the chained block's row count can be counted without a second rank."
        },
        "pcoh_ordinal": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "ratecohort_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Its own position, 1..n_runspecpollutantprocess x n_agecategory x n_runspecsourcefueltype. This relation is the pollutant-process crossed with the COHORT candidate grid -- the rate relation with the source bin divided out -- and it exists because the output relation is keyed by (block, cohort) and the rate relation is keyed by (block, BIN). J78."
        },
        "pcoh_polProcOrdinal": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "ratecohort_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Which pollutant-process this (block, cohort) pair is for. Pollutant-process major, the same order `rate_rows` uses, which is what makes the rank below agree with the emitted row order."
        },
        "pcoh_cohortOrdinal": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "ratecohort_rows"
          ],
          "description": "And which cohort candidate, 1..n_agecategory x n_runspecsourcefueltype."
        },
        "pcoh_outputBlockOrdinal": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "ratecohort_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The output block this pair's pollutant-process owns, read back from `rspp_outputOrdinal`. 0 on every pair of a pollutant-process that is not emitted."
        },
        "pcoh_hasEmittingRate": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "ratecohort_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Does ANY rate row of this (pollutant-process, cohort) pair put a number in MOVESOutput? The OR over the cohort's source bins of `rt_emits`. It is NOT the same question as whether the pair is emitted: six of `expand-criteria`'s eight selected pollutant-processes have rate rows that emit and only three of them happen on a road, so this counts 744 pairs where the run emits 372."
        },
        "pcoh_emits": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "ratecohort_rows"
          ],
          "description": "And is its pollutant-process an emitted BLOCK? A pair emits when a rate row of it emits AND the road-type test kept its block -- the two conditions the old `(block - 1) x n_outputCohort + cohortRank` packing carried in its `and`, which is why they have to be spelled again here."
        },
        "pcoh_prefixEmits": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "ratecohort_rows"
          ],
          "description": "An inclusive prefix count of `rc_emits` over the whole pollutant-process-major relation (docs/esm-conventions.md §22)."
        },
        "pcoh_rateOrdinal": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "ratecohort_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The prefix count ZEROED on the pairs that do not emit, so every emitted (block, cohort) pair has a dense 1..n_outputRate and every other pair has 0. This is the output relation's leading key and it replaces the (block - 1) x n_outputCohort + cohortRank packing, which could only number a rectangle."
        },
        "pcoh_cohortRank": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "ratecohort_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The cohort's GLOBAL dense rank, 1..cohortSurvivorCount, which `coh_rank` is the other side of. The output row reads its model year, fuel type and source type from the cohort relation through this, not through the rate ordinal, because a key column is read from the relation that OWNS it."
        },
        "rateSurvivorCount": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "run_rows"
          ],
          "description": "How many (pollutant-process, cohort) pairs emit, computed from the chain, so `n_outputRate` is a CHECKED declaration rather than a transcription. It equals `outputPolProcessCount` x `cohortSurvivorCount` exactly when the run is a rectangle, and is LESS when a chained block is narrower than its parent; run-tests.sh prints which."
        },
        "rt_outputOrdinal": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "rate_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Where this rate row lands in the output relation: the dense rank of its (pollutant-process, cohort) PAIR, read from `rc_rateOrdinal`, and 0 when that pair does not emit. Every source bin of the same cohort carries the same ordinal and `out_emissionQuant` sums over them, which is what it did before; what changed at J78 is that the number is a rank and no longer (block - 1) x n_outputCohort + rank, an arithmetic that assumed every block is the same width."
        },
        "outPolProcOrdinal": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "output_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Which output block this row belongs to, read BACK from the pair relation through the rate rank. It used to be a division of the row index by a fixed block length; a block length is exactly what a ragged output does not have (J78)."
        },
        "outRateOrdinal": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "output_rows"
          ],
          "description": "S17's row structure: the output relation is (pollutant-process, cohort) PAIR major, then county, then month, then day type. This is the pair's dense rank, 1..n_outputRate, and `rc_rateOrdinal` is the other side of it. The county, month and day levels moved INSIDE the pair at J78; nothing reads the emitted order but `process-tirewear`'s byte-for-byte check, whose run has one county, one month and one day type, so its rows are where they were."
        },
        "outRateOffset": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "output_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The row's offset within its pair's block of counties x months x day types, which the three levels below decompose in that order."
        },
        "outCountyOrdinal": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "output_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Which of the run's counties this output row is for. The output relation is pollutant-process major, then COUNTY, then day type, then cohort; `expand-counties` is the run that makes this level wider than one."
        },
        "outCountyOffset": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "output_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The position of this row within its county's block, which is what the day and cohort levels decompose."
        },
        "outMonthOrdinal": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "output_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Month-major within the county's block."
        },
        "outDayOrdinal": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "output_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Day-major within the county's block."
        },
        "outCohortRank": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "output_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The surviving cohort's GLOBAL rank, 1..cohortSurvivorCount, which `coh_rank` is the other side of. It is read back from the pair relation rather than decomposed out of the row index, because the pair's position in its block is not the cohort's rank once a block is narrower than the widest one."
        },
        "out_modelYearID": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "output_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The output row's model year, read BACK through the rank join. A KEY column is read from the relation that OWNS it -- this one from the cohort relation through the rank, not from the rate relation through `rt_outputOrdinal` -- because since 7e several rate rows can share an output row and a `sum_product` over them would add their keys together. The VALUE columns below keep the rate join, where that sum is the point."
        },
        "out_fuelTypeID": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "output_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Its fuel type. A KEY column is read from the relation that OWNS it -- this one from the cohort relation through the rank, not from the rate relation through `rt_outputOrdinal` -- because since 7e several rate rows can share an output row and a `sum_product` over them would add their keys together. The VALUE columns below keep the rate join, where that sum is the point."
        },
        "out_pollutantID": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "output_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Its pollutant: 91, 107 or 117. A KEY column is read from the relation that OWNS it -- this one from the cohort relation through the rank, not from the rate relation through `rt_outputOrdinal` -- because since 7e several rate rows can share an output row and a `sum_product` over them would add their keys together. The VALUE columns below keep the rate join, where that sum is the point."
        },
        "out_processID": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "output_rows"
          ],
          "description": "Its process: 1 or 9. A KEY column is read from the relation that OWNS it -- this one from the cohort relation through the rank, not from the rate relation through `rt_outputOrdinal` -- because since 7e several rate rows can share an output row and a `sum_product` over them would add their keys together. The VALUE columns below keep the rate join, where that sum is the point."
        },
        "out_meanBaseRate": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "output_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The collapsed base rate on the output row. Emitted as an extra column: the comparator ignores columns MOVESOutput does not have, and having it in the CSV is what makes a disagreement attributable to the rate or to the activity."
        },
        "out_adjustedMeanBaseRate": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "output_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The same after S15."
        },
        "out_emissionQuantDirect": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "output_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The row's own rate path, before the chain -- 0 on every PM10 row."
        },
        "out_emissionQuant": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "shape": [
            "output_rows"
          ],
          "description": "S18: Million BTU for pollutant 91 and grams for 107 and 117. THE compared column."
        }
      },
