        {
          "id": "the_collapsed_base_rate_reproduces_both_of_the_references_generator_tables",
          "description": "`rtDay_meanBaseRate` IS `baserate_1_2020.meanBaseRate` on the 9101 rows and `baserate_10_2020.meanBaseRate` on the 11710 rows -- the reference's own S14 intermediates, computed here. It is the most direct check of the weights available, and it is the check this fixture adds, because THE TWO BLOCKS ARE CONTRACTED AGAINST DIFFERENT WEIGHT VECTORS. The 9101 cells collapse 23 drive-cycle operating modes; the 11710 cells collapse 16 speed-bin modes that no drive schedule ever produces. A document that used one vector for both -- the reflex, since the two mode families are disjoint in `emissionrate` and a union would still give the right answer here -- would reproduce all ten of these cells and would be writing down a coincidence. What it would not reproduce is `pp_WModeCount`, which is why that assertion sits beside these.\n\nThe two rate tables are seven orders of magnitude apart -- 392,141 kJ per source-hour against 0.0000197 g -- so a wrong weight is a wrong number in every one of these cells and no shared scale factor can hide behind it.\n\n`rtDay_meanBaseRateACAdj` is the INCREMENTAL A/C load of sbweighted.rs:459. It reaches emissionQuant multiplied by an A/C factor this document computes to be exactly 0, so the OUTPUT cannot see it -- but the generator tables carry it in their own column, so it is checked here anyway, on both paths: 30,166.7 for 9101 and an exact 0 for 11710, which is what an absent `fullacadjustment` row has to produce.\n\nThe tolerance is the reference's six-significant-figure column storage.",
